  "description": "Real names that mean fire, flame or hearth, from Aidan and Seraphina to Azar and Keahi, plus popular 'fire' names that don't mean fire at all.",
  "section": "meanings",
  "published": "2026-11-13",
  "motif": "flame",
  "intro": "<p>The best-known fire name in England and Wales is hiding in plain sight: Aidan, and its spelling Aiden, come from the Old Irish <em>Áedán</em>, 'little fire'. This list gathers real names like it from Irish, Persian, Turkish, Sanskrit, Slavic, Armenian, Hawaiian and other languages, each meaning checked against a cited source, mostly Behind the Name and Wiktionary. Ranks for England and Wales come from the ONS.</p><p>Fire attracts more myths than most meanings. Bridget is often called 'fiery arrow', Ignatius is linked to Latin <em>ignis</em>, and Blaise is assumed to mean blaze. None of those holds up, and we've marked them uncertain with the real story.</p>",
