  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "The Aodh family",
      "content": "<p>Old Irish <em>áed</em>, 'fire', gave Ireland one of its oldest names, Aodh, and a string of diminutives: Aodhán ('little fire'), which became Aidan, and Aodhagán, which survives in the surname Keegan. If you like Aidan but want something less common, Aodhán is the Irish original. It sounds much the same, roughly AY-dawn, but people outside Ireland may need help with the spelling.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Word names",
      "content": "<p>Ember, Phoenix and Blaze are English word names, not traditional ones, and all three are used for boys and girls in England and Wales. They're 'built from real words', which is why they're marked derived rather than recorded. Phoenix sits well with almost any surname; Ember and Blaze are short enough to pair with a longer surname.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch the spelling",
      "content": "<p>Suzan, the Turkish name meaning 'burning', is a different name from Susan. Nina only means fire in Quechua and Aymara. If the meaning matters, make sure the name you choose really comes from the language that gives it, and tell people which one you mean.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What boy name means fire?",
      "answer": "Aidan and Aiden ('little fire', from Irish Aodhán) are the most popular. Others include Aodh and Keegan (Irish), Agni (Sanskrit), Ateş and Alaz (Turkish), and Plamen and Ognyan (Bulgarian)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What girl name means fire?",
      "answer": "Seraphina comes from seraphim, 'fiery ones'. Others include Hestia ('hearth'), Fiamma (Italian, 'flame'), Alev (Turkish, 'flame'), Brenda (Old Norse, 'fire, torch, sword') and Ugnė (Lithuanian, 'fire')."
    },
    {
      "question": "What name means fire in Greek?",
      "answer": "Pyrrhus ('flame-coloured', from pyr, fire) and Phlegon ('burning'). Hestia means hearth. Seraphina is often given as Greek but comes from Hebrew via Latin."
    }
  ],
